    card title "How much rice on the last square?"

A: An old legend says a king offered the inventor of chess any reward he liked. He asked for rice.
    draw board

A: One grain on the first square, two on the second, four on the third, doubling all the way to the sixty-fourth.
    draw n1 n2 n3 n4 n5

B: That sounds modest. A few sacks, tops?
    draw lit64 q64 grains

    ask "The last square alone: how many years of the whole world's rice harvest would it hold? Click on the line." on grains store g default "0.01"
        right: Close: you said {g}, and it is about {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it is about {g.true}.

A: Nearly three centuries of harvests, on that one square.
    highlight grains_answer

A: It gets two to the power sixty-three grains: about nine quintillion.
    draw big

B: Wait. How heavy is nine quintillion grains?
    highlight lit64

A: At about twenty-five milligrams a grain, two hundred and thirty billion tonnes. The world grows about eight hundred million tonnes a year.
    draw tonnes

B: So the first squares fooled the king.
    point at.ref n1 gesture underline

A: Exactly. The first twenty squares together hold about one sack, some twenty-five kilos.
    draw first20 sack sack_arrow

A: It is the last doublings that do the damage: each square holds more than all the squares before it put together.
    highlight lit64 effect circle

B: Remind me never to let you set my salary.
    highlight tonnes

